platform/chrome: cros_ec_typec: Register notifier before initial update

Mode selection can issue EC commands while processing an already attached
partner during the first port update. Those commands generate PD or mux
events, but probe currently performs the update before registering its
notifier. The event can then be lost until the transition timeout.

Register the notifier first and queue the initial update through the same
work item used for later events. An event arriving between notifier
registration and the explicit queue operation is naturally coalesced, and
event-driven updates remain serialized.

An initial EC read failure then follows the same path as a later failure:
the worker reports it, and a later notification can recover without
rebinding the driver.

---
diff --git a/drivers/platform/chrome/cros_ec_typec.c b/drivers/platform/chrome/cros_ec_typec.c
--- a/drivers/platform/chrome/cros_ec_typec.c
+++ b/drivers/platform/chrome/cros_ec_typec.c
@@ -1383,7 +1383,7 @@
 	struct device *dev = &pdev->dev;
 	struct cros_typec_data *typec;
 	struct ec_response_usb_pd_ports resp;
-	int ret, i;
+	int ret;
 
 	typec = devm_kzalloc(dev, sizeof(*typec), GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!typec)
@@ -1433,21 +1433,13 @@
 
 	INIT_WORK(&typec->port_work, cros_typec_port_work);
 
-	/*
-	 * Safe to call port update here, since we haven't registered the
-	 * PD notifier yet.
-	 */
-	for (i = 0; i < typec->num_ports; i++) {
-		ret = cros_typec_port_update(typec, i);
-		if (ret < 0)
-			goto unregister_ports;
-	}
-
 	typec->nb.notifier_call = cros_ec_typec_event;
 	ret = cros_usbpd_register_notify(&typec->nb);
 	if (ret < 0)
 		goto unregister_ports;
 
+	schedule_work(&typec->port_work);
+
 	return 0;
 
 unregister_ports:
-- 
2.54.0
